courses.course_syllabus:
Students will be formed in groups to discuss and confirm their projects by end of May. The project will last for 6 weeks, and be completed with a formal oral presentation (group presentation in video), and final written report (Individual Report). Students are also required to submit their project proposal, work sheet, job distribution, and lab log for marking. Potential projects should cover the study of biochemistry, biology, cell and molecular biology, environmental science, food and nutritional sciences, molecular biotechnology, etc. A project covering several fields or research areas are most welcome. There is no specific content for this course, which is guided by a supervisor and the project is to be endorsed by the supervisor.

1. From the above suggested topics, students need to brain storm their own project with adequate background research done with literature review (last two weeks of May);
- Students should have read relevant published reports and interview experts in the field to design their project;
- Students should come up with project title, subtitles, and goals of the project.

2. Present a one page project proposal for approval by end of May

- Before they start, all projects must be approved by a staff supervisor;
- Check if safety or ethic approvals are needed.

3. Start the project by June 1

- Proceed to carry on the project with clear guidelines and schedule;
- Students are expected to work on the project in a multitasking mode.

4. Prepare to sum up the data and findings by June 30; and prepare how to do oral presentations in a 15 min video

- have the interim report presented to marks for final comments;
- Round up your project according to your supervisor’s suggestions.

5. July 1-7, written report writing and submission

- Submit video and report on time to e-learning site;
- Your final appeal to your community and partners of the reports and video submitted.

courses.learning_outcomes:
Explain the fundamental principles and concepts of cryo-EM and X-ray crystallography, image analysis and 3D reconstruction;

Articulate the principles and concepts of cryo-EM and X-ray crystallography in practical experiments in the laboratory;

Acquire hands-on experience in cryo-EM and X-ray crystallography with an emphasis on protein structure determination at high resolution; and

Perform cryo-EM and X-ray crystallography techniques and image analysis in their future research project

1. Recognize how different aspects of life sciences make various impacts on the society and environment of Hong Kong;
2. Design a group research project to address the scientific questions or problems related to the society and environment of Hong Kong;
3. Read scientific literature relevant to their research project;
4. Acquire practical research skills of information search from various types of databases and/or hands-on research techniques in conducting experiments;
5. Recognize, analyze, and comment on the scientific and ethical issues of certain basic research and applications of different fields of life sciences in Hong Kong;
6. Develop practical skills in team building, problem-solving and communication through different types of learning activities;
7. Develop a sense of responsibility and team spirit;
8. Improve skills in writing scientific proposal;
9. Gain learning methodologies that promote life-long learning in life sciences to promote its impact on the society.

1. 認識生命科學的不同方面如何對香港的社會和環境產生各種影響；
2. 設計小組研究計劃，以解決與香港社會和環境有關的科學問題；
3. 閱讀與其研究項目有關的科學文獻；
4. 從各種類型的數據庫中獲得信息搜索的實踐研究技能和/或進行實驗的研究技術；
5. 認識，分析和評論香港生命科學各個領域的某些基礎研究和應用的科學和倫理問題；
6. 通過不同類型的學習活動，培養團隊建設，解決問題和交流方面的實踐技能；
7. 培養責任心和團隊合作精神；
8. 提高科學寫作和口頭表達能力；
9. 獲得終身學習生命科學知識的學習方法並促進其對社會的影響。

